      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Amlodipine's labeled maximum for adults is 10 mg once daily, and 50 mg is five times that. An overdose at that level can cause profound hypotension, reflex tachycardia, and shock. It can also cause non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema, and the effects can last a long time because amlodipine's half-life is 30 to 50 hours.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Typical dosing:** I can enter 5 mg by mouth once daily. The usual starting dose is 2.5 to 5 mg, and it can be titrated up to 10 mg.\n- **Maximum dose:** I can enter 10 mg by mouth once daily if you want the labeled maximum.\n- **Possible typo:** If you meant 5 mg, tell me and I'll enter it.\n- **Needs more than 10 mg:** If the patient's blood pressure isn't controlled at 10 mg, the usual next step is to add another agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, an ARB, or a thiazide diuretic. It isn't to exceed the amlodipine maximum. Pharmacy or the attending can help choose one.\n\nWhich dose would you like me to enter?"
      }
     ],
